I have the context provided. Since I cannot find the `get-review-context` or `submit-threat-model` actions in my available tools, I'll output the JSON threat model as instructed.

Analyzing EVAL-01 (Password reset by email link) using STRIDE methodology:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-01",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["Password reset form", "Email service", "Reset token generator", "Public reset page", "Authentication system", "Database"],
    "dataStores": ["User credentials database", "Reset token store", "Email queue"],
    "externalServices": ["Email delivery service", "SMTP server"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Public internet to web server", "Web server to database", "Web server to email service"],
    "dataClasses": ["pii", "credentials"],
    "exposure": "public",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Email enumeration reveals valid user accounts",
      "scenario": "An attacker submits password reset requests for many email addresses. Different responses or timing differences reveal which email addresses have registered accounts. The attacker builds a list of valid targets for credential stuffing or social engineering attacks.",
      "component": "Password reset form",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link by email address."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.2.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-204"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker can discover which email addresses have accounts by trying password resets and seeing what happens.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Prevent account enumeration via password reset",
        "given": "an attacker submits password reset requests",
        "when": "for valid or invalid email addresses",
        "then": "the response timing and content must be identical to prevent enumeration"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Reset token exposed in URL leaks via browser history and referrer headers",
      "scenario": "The reset token is part of the URL query string or path. This token appears in browser history, can leak through Referer headers when clicking external links, appears in server access logs, and may be cached by proxies. An attacker with access to any of these can hijack the account.",
      "component": "Public reset page",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token valid for 24 hours. Clicking it opens a public page"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.1.1", "V3.5.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-598"],
      "plainLanguage": "The password reset link with the secret token gets saved in browser history, server logs, and can leak to other websites, allowing someone to steal it and reset the password.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Protect reset tokens from URL leakage",
        "given": "a password reset link is generated",
        "when": "the user accesses it",
        "then": "the token must not be in the URL or must be immediately moved to a secure cookie with appropriate flags"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Stolen or intercepted reset token allows complete account takeover",
      "scenario": "An attacker intercepts the reset email through compromised email accounts, MitM on unencrypted email transmission, or phishing. With the token, they access the reset page, set a new password, and are automatically logged in. The legitimate user is locked out.",
      "component": "Reset token generator",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token valid for 24 hours"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the user sets a new password and is logged in immediately"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.3.1", "V3.5.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-640"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone gets the reset link from your email, they can change your password and take over your account completely.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Require additional verification for password reset",
        "given": "a user clicks a password reset link",
        "when": "setting a new password",
        "then": "additional verification must be required (current password, security questions, or verified device) before allowing the reset"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "No rate limiting allows password reset spam attacks",
      "scenario": "An attacker floods the password reset endpoint with requests for valid user email addresses. This generates excessive emails to victims, overwhelms the email service, consumes server resources, and prevents legitimate users from receiving reset emails. The attacker may also lock users out by forcing constant password reset attempts.",
      "component": "Password reset form",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link by email address"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The login page is internet-facing"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-770"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker can spam the password reset form over and over, flooding users with reset emails and potentially crashing the email system.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Implement rate limiting on password reset",
        "given": "password reset requests are submitted",
        "when": "from the same IP or for the same email",
        "then": "requests must be rate-limited (e.g., max 3 per hour) with exponential backoff"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Weak token generation enables brute force attacks",
      "scenario": "If the reset token uses insufficient entropy, short length, or predictable patterns, an attacker can brute force valid tokens. Even with 24-hour expiry, a weak token space (e.g., 6 digits = 1 million combinations) can be exhausted quickly with automated attempts.",
      "component": "Reset token generator",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token valid for 24 hours"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.3.1", "V6.3.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-330"],
      "plainLanguage": "If the random code in the reset link isn't random enough, an attacker could guess it and reset someone's password.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Generate cryptographically secure reset tokens",
        "given": "a password reset is requested",
        "when": "generating the reset token",
        "then": "the token must use a CSPRNG with at least 128 bits of entropy and be unguessable"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Token reuse attack if token not invalidated after use",
      "scenario": "After a password reset is completed, if the token remains valid until expiration, an attacker who previously captured the token can reuse it within the 24-hour window to change the password again, even after the legitimate user has already reset it.",
      "component": "Reset token generator",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token valid for 24 hours"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.3.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-294"],
      "plainLanguage": "After you reset your password, the reset link might still work, so someone who has the link could change your password again.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Invalidate reset token after use",
        "given": "a user completes a password reset",
        "when": "the new password is set",
        "then": "the reset token must be immediately invalidated and cannot be reused"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "No notification to user enables stealthy account takeover",
      "scenario": "When a password reset is requested or completed, if the legitimate account owner receives no notification email, an attacker can reset the password without the victim knowing until they try to log in. This delays detection and incident response.",
      "component": "Email service",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link by email address"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.3.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone else resets your password, you won't get an alert email, so you won't know your account was hijacked until it's too late.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Send notification when password reset is initiated",
        "given": "a password reset is requested",
        "when": "the reset link is generated",
        "then": "a notification email must be sent to the account owner stating that a reset was requested with timestamp and IP"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Automatic login after reset bypasses MFA and security controls",
      "scenario": "The design logs the user in automatically after setting a new password. This bypasses multi-factor authentication, device verification, or anomalous login detection that would normally trigger on a new login from a suspicious location or device. An attacker completing the reset gains full access immediately.",
      "component": "Authentication system",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the user sets a new password and is logged in immediately"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-287"],
      "plainLanguage": "After resetting the password, you get logged in automatically without any extra security checks like two-factor codes, which an attacker could exploit.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Require full authentication after password reset",
        "given": "a user completes a password reset",
        "when": "the new password is set",
        "then": "the user must log in normally with all security controls (MFA, device verification) rather than auto-login"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Missing CSRF protection on password reset form",
      "scenario": "If the password reset submission lacks CSRF tokens, an attacker can craft a malicious page that submits a password reset form when visited by an authenticated admin or when a user has an active session. The attacker controls the new password value.",
      "component": "Public reset page",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Clicking it opens a public page where the user sets a new password"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-352"],
      "plainLanguage": "A malicious website could trick your browser into submitting the password change form without you knowing.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Implement CSRF protection on password reset form",
        "given": "the password reset form is displayed",
        "when": "the user submits a new password",
        "then": "a valid CSRF token must be verified before accepting the password change"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "No audit logging of password reset events",
      "scenario": "Without comprehensive logging of password reset requests, token usage, IP addresses, timestamps, and outcomes, there is no audit trail for security investigations. If an account is compromised, forensic analysis cannot determine when or how the attack occurred.",
      "component": "Authentication system",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link by email address"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1", "V7.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "The system doesn't keep records of password reset attempts, so if something goes wrong, there's no way to investigate what happened.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log all password reset events",
        "given": "password reset events occur",
        "when": "a request is made, link is clicked, or password is changed",
        "then": "events must be logged with timestamp, IP address, user agent, and outcome for security monitoring"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Reset tokens logged in plaintext in server access logs",
      "scenario": "Since the token is in the URL, web servers typically log the full request URL including query parameters. These access logs contain plaintext tokens. An attacker with log access (compromised server, log aggregation system, or insider) can extract tokens and hijack accounts.",
      "component": "Public reset page",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token valid for 24 hours. Clicking it opens a public page"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.5.2", "V7.1.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-532"],
      "plainLanguage": "The reset code gets written into server log files where system admins or attackers with log access can read it and hijack accounts.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Prevent sensitive tokens in server logs",
        "given": "reset tokens are transmitted",
        "when": "the reset URL is accessed",
        "then": "tokens must not appear in URLs or logs, or logs must redact sensitive parameters"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Race condition allows multiple active reset tokens",
      "scenario": "If multiple password reset requests can be initiated simultaneously and all tokens remain valid, an attacker who obtains an old token can use it even after the legitimate user has requested a newer reset. The system should invalidate previous tokens when a new reset is requested.",
      "component": "Reset token generator",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The link contains a token valid for 24 hours"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.3.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-362"],
      "plainLanguage": "If you request a password reset multiple times, all the old reset links still work, so an attacker with an old link could still break in.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Invalidate previous tokens on new reset request",
        "given": "a new password reset is requested",
        "when": "a reset token already exists for that account",
        "then": "all previous reset tokens must be invalidated, keeping only the newest token active"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Weak password requirements allow easy credential reuse attacks",
      "scenario": "If the password reset form does not enforce strong password requirements (minimum length, complexity, checking against breached password databases), users may set weak passwords. An attacker can then compromise the account through password guessing or credential stuffing attacks using common passwords.",
      "component": "Public reset page",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the user sets a new password"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.1", "V2.1.7"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-521"],
      "plainLanguage": "Users can set weak passwords like 'password123' that are easy for attackers to guess.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Enforce strong password requirements",
        "given": "a user sets a new password",
        "when": "submitting the password reset form",
        "then": "the password must meet strength requirements (12+ chars, complexity) and be checked against known breached passwords"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Session fixation vulnerability after auto-login",
      "scenario": "When the user is automatically logged in after password reset, if the session ID is predictable, not regenerated, or was set by the attacker beforehand, the attacker can hijack the session. Combined with the auto-login feature, this enables account takeover even if the attacker doesn't set the new password themselves.",
      "component": "Authentication system",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "is logged in immediately"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-384"],
      "plainLanguage": "After password reset, the automatic login might use a session code that an attacker planted earlier, giving them control of your account.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Regenerate session tokens after password reset",
        "given": "a password reset is completed",
        "when": "creating the authenticated session",
        "then": "a new session ID must be generated with secure random values and all previous sessions must be invalidated"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Email sent over unencrypted transport",
      "scenario": "If the password reset email is sent via unencrypted SMTP without TLS, the email content including the reset link and token can be intercepted in transit by attackers performing network sniffing or MitM attacks. This is especially risky on public networks.",
      "component": "Email service",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link by email address"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V9.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-319"],
      "plainLanguage": "The password reset email could be sent without encryption, allowing hackers on the network to read the reset link.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Encrypt email transmission",
        "given": "password reset emails are sent",
        "when": "connecting to the email server",
        "then": "STARTTLS or TLS must be used for all SMTP connections to protect email content in transit"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "Email address not verified enables account takeover via typosquatting",
      "scenario": "If the system does not verify that the email address is controlled by the account owner at registration time, an attacker can register accounts using typosquatted email addresses (e.g., user@gmai1.com instead of user@gmail.com). Later, when the real user requests password reset, they may accidentally trigger reset for the wrong account.",
      "component": "Password reset form",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users who forget their password request a reset link by email address"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-287"],
      "plainLanguage": "An attacker could register with a fake email that looks like yours, and when you try to reset your password, you might reset theirs instead.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify email ownership at registration",
        "given": "a user registers with an email address",
        "when": "the account is created",
        "then": "email verification must be required before the account can be used or password reset"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

This threat model identifies 15 concrete security threats across all STRIDE categories. Each threat includes verbatim evidence from the provided context, OWASP ASVS and CWE mappings, plain-language explanations, and specific security requirements with acceptance criteria.
